drake references: bare means own package, qualified means elsewhere (#54, #56)

A `from` / `modules` reference is spelled BARE when it lives in the referring type's own
package and QUALIFIED (lower-case package words, then the name) everywhere else.

WHY NOT JUST QUALIFY EVERYTHING, which is what #54 proposed and what I was about to
build. Measured first, over all 112 references:

    67  same package as the referring type      draco.Action -> draco.BodyElement
    27  draco, the root domain                  draco.base.Cardinal -> draco.Primal
    17  genuinely elsewhere                     domains.marine.FixReport -> draco.format.json.JSON
     1  no package at all                       draco.Dictionary -> Map

84% are reconstructable, so blanket qualification would have added package words to the
majority of references purely to restate the package we are already in — exactly what
drake.dlt INFERENCE forbids, and the same principle that justified dropping
Condition.parameters and the pinned valueTypes in the two commits before this. The
stronger rule (own package, then fall back to draco) would leave 94 bare and qualify
only 17, but "is there such a type in my package?" is a dictionary question and
Drake.parse has no dictionary. Bare-means-own-package needs no lookup: the parser
already knows the owning package from the `domain` line.

Result: 67 references unchanged, 44 gained package words across 19 files.

THE SEQUENCE NEEDS NO SEPARATOR, which was #56's open worry — that package-qualifying a
`from` reference would make the lower-case package words indistinguishable from the
sequence's own structure. It does not: case bounds each member and therefore the run.
Lower-case words are package, the first upper-case token ends the reference, the next
lower-case word begins the following one. `from Unit draco Primal(T)` is a bare
reference followed by a qualified one; `from draco Dictionary(K, V) draco DracoType` is
two qualified ones. Verified against all 22 multi-element derivations and all 39
parameterized references. It is the same rule that already bounded
`extensible org apache pekko actor typed ExtensibleBehavior(T)` — #56's other item was
that this convention was unstated, and drake.dlt now has a REFERENCES section stating it.

`domain` / `super` / `extensible` are untouched and stay unconditionally qualified: each
names something OUTSIDE the type being declared, so there is no own-package to infer
against.

Resolution happens when the TypeDefinition is assembled, not at the reference, because
`from` precedes `domain` on the surface and the owning package is not known until the
whole source has been read.

THE RESIDUAL, and it is a different problem from the one #54's title tracks. A reference
with NO package at all — a host type outside every draco domain, `Map` in Dictionary
being the corpus's only one — is spelled bare and so returns owning the referring
package. #54 was "the surface loses the package"; this is "the surface cannot express
the ABSENCE of a package". Recorded in drake.dlt REFERENCES and measured by the loss
report rather than hidden.

That is also why DrakeParseTest's surfaceCarried still normalizes derivation/modules
packages away. Drop that normalization and gate 2 verifies packages outright — it would
pass for every definition except Dictionary. Settling `Map` (giving it a real package
touches the host-reserved-name question, since Scala's is scala.collection.immutable.Map)
is a decision about corpus data, left to Dev.

Corpus rewritten by regenerating the two affected line kinds from the definitions.
BodyElement.drake was left alone: its modules block closes with a glued `Local ]` rather
than a lone bracket, it is hand-authored ahead of the JSON so no gate compares it, and
its modules are all same-package so the rule would not have changed a character.
EOF

